    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense for the originalists, but it seems like you're framing their approach as more \"objective\" and \"consistent\" compared to the living constitutionalist side. If the living constitutionalist approach is based on \"subjective judicial philosophy\" as you imply, how can it be considered a legitimate or stable way to interpret the Constitution? Doesn't that approach essentially just allow judges to do whatever they want under the guise of \"modernity\"?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Living constitutionalism is not a \"subjective philosophy\"; it is a pragmatic recognition that governing documents must remain relevant to a changing society. While originalism seeks to freeze the past, the living constitutionalist approach allows the Constitution to function as a living framework for justice in the modern world. To claim judges are simply \"doing whatever they want\" ignores the rigorous, evolving legal arguments that underpin modern interpretations of rights.",
      "source": "target",
